<section class="section section--tight" aria-labelledby="pair-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="pair-title">The price of a pair decides the rate</h2>
      <p>The GST Council recommended, with effect from 22 September 2025, cutting the rate on footwear of sale value not exceeding ₹2,500 per pair from 12% to 5%, and keeping footwear above ₹2,500 per pair at 18%. Check the notification in force before you bill. What follows is our general analysis of the records this calls for.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Records for pricing footwear per pair, scrolls sideways on small screens">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">What to record</th><th scope="col">Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Quantity in pairs on every invoice line</th><td>The limit is tested per pair, so cartons or dozens alone do not show it</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Value per pair for each article</th><td>Shows which side of ₹2,500 each line falls, and that the right rate was charged</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Articles priced close to ₹2,500</th><td>A price rise, discount or scheme can move them across the line. Ask how discounts affect the value per pair</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Orders billed around 22 September 2025</th><td>Two rates applied either side of that date, so check each invoice used the one in force</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="credit-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="credit-title">When credit builds up</h2>
      <p>If the tax on your materials is higher than the 5% you charge, credit can pile up faster than you can use it. Section 54(3) of the CGST Act allows a refund of unused input tax credit where it has built up because the rate on inputs is higher than the rate on output supplies, other than nil-rated or fully exempt supplies, and except for supplies the government notifies.</p>
      <p class="para">Rule 89(5) of the CGST Rules works out that refund from credit on inputs only. Credit on services, such as rent, repairs or transport, sits outside the calculation. So keep the two apart in your books from the start, rather than sorting them out when you claim.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Before you claim</h2>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Confirm the rates you actually paid on your main materials, from the bills</li>
        <li>Check that footwear is not among the supplies notified as excluded</li>
        <li>Make sure purchases are reflected properly in your returns before you count on the credit</li>
        <li>Ask about provisional refunds: the Council recommended 90% on a risk basis for these claims, so check how that now works</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="para">The copies of the Act and Rules we read are dated, so ask your adviser to check the current wording.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="stock-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="stock-title">Stock in pairs, sizes and kilos</h2>
      <p>Compound and granules are bought by weight, soles and uppers by piece, and finished goods sold in pairs and size sets. Your stock records need a way to move between these.</p>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>A normal yield, in pairs per kilo of compound, for your main articles</li>
        <li>Finished stock counted by article and size, not only in cartons</li>
        <li>Rejects and seconds recorded separately, with what they sold for</li>
        <li>Odd sizes left from broken sets reviewed, since they may be worth less than cost</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Moulds and machines</h2>
      <p>Each article and size can need its own mould. Moulds cost real money and wear out, so list them in your fixed asset register with the article they make, rather than writing them off as expenses. When a design is dropped, note which moulds are no longer used.</p>
      <p class="para">The rest of a unit's workflow, from material issue to job work, is on {% routeLink "/industries/accounting-for-manufacturers/", "accounting for manufacturers" %}.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="labour-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="labour-title">Workers paid through contractors</h2>
      <p>A 2022 study of footwear workshops in Narela by researchers at O.P. Jindal Global University found workers paid daily, weekly, monthly or on contract. It described labour contractors who supply and organise workers for a commission.</p>
      <p class="para">If you pay a contractor, keep their bill, the attendance behind it and proof of payment by bank. Ask your accountant whether tax should be deducted from the payment, and who is responsible for PF and ESI. See {% routeLink "/services/payroll-services/", "payroll" %}.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2 id="fire-title">If stock is lost in a fire</h2>
      <p>Press reports describe several fires at footwear units in Narela, in 2022, 2025 and 2026. This page does not suggest anything about any particular business. A loss is far easier to deal with if your records survive it.</p>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Keep stock records and bills backed up away from the factory</li>
        <li>Compare the insured value with your actual stock from time to time</li>
        <li>Keep the fire service and surveyor's reports with the loss entry</li>
        <li>Ask about credit already taken: section 17(5)(h) of the CGST Act blocks credit on goods destroyed or written off</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="us-title">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h2 id="us-title" class="first-h2">About the area, and about us</h2>
    <p>Delhi's Department of Industries lists Narela in its North Zone. If your question is about a plot under the relocation scheme rather than your product, see {% routeLink "/locations/accounting-services-in-bawana-industrial-area/", "the Bawana page" %}, as the same questions apply in Narela. AccountOrbit has no office in Narela, and takes enquiries by phone from anywhere in Delhi. For a footwear unit, your price list per article and a recent month's purchase bills are the most useful things to have to hand on the first call.</p>
  </div>
</section>

